[SIZE=5][B]What works[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*]Android TV home screen and setup with the stock [B]infrared remote[/B] (stock key table), Play Store (TV)
[*]Wi-Fi 5 GHz (AP6275S); v2/v3 tested with sustained 8 GB transfers. Bluetooth
[*]Hardware video decoding H.264 / HEVC / VP9 — YouTube and SmartTube play 4K60 VP9 without dropped frames
[*][B]HDMI / S-PDIF / analog audio[/B] (L-PCM; fixed in v3). Tested on a 1080p60 TV; 4K output modes and HDMI-CEC TV control are present but untested — reports welcome
[*]USB host (keyboards, mice, gamepads, sticks; in v2/v3 hot-plug also works after standby), microSD, RTC
[*]Thermal management on both CPU clusters; in v2/v3 the GPU clock is pinned for games
[*]Standby: power key = screen off, instant wake with the remote
[*]ADB over network
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]v1 (32-bit) vs v3 (64-bit)[/B][/SIZE]
Measured on the test unit (1080p60 TV):
[TABLE]
[TR][TH][/TH][TH]v1 (32-bit)[/TH][TH]v3 (64-bit)[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]PlayStation 2[/TD][TD]not possible (no 32-bit PS2 emulator)[/TD][TD]ARMSX2: God of War (PAL) 50/50 fps, 16:9 and no-interlace patches, 8x AF, sharpening[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]GameCube / Wii[/TD][TD]not possible (Dolphin is 64-bit only)[/TD][TD]Dolphin installs (not tested with a game)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]PlayStation 1[/TD][TD]PCSX-ReARMed, native resolution[/TD][TD]SwanStation 5x (1080p), PGXP, 24-bit colour, 16:9 — 50/50 fps[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]PSP[/TD][TD]32-bit libretro core[/TD][TD]PPSSPP (Vulkan) 2x — God of War: Ghost of Sparta 60/60 fps[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]GPU driver[/TD][TD]Mali r32p1, 32-bit[/TD][TD]Mali r51p0, lib64, Vulkan 1.3, GLES 3.2[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]
Honest notes: the main gain of the 64-bit build is what cannot run on 32-bit at all (a PS2 emulator reserves ~8 GB of address space, Dolphin is arm64-only). There is no same-game fps comparison between v1 and v3. The PSP result owes a lot to the standalone Vulkan PPSSPP. PS2 at full speed also needs the Mali clock pinned at 800 MHz (v3 does this at boot). PS2 upscaling is beyond the Mali-G52 — games run at native resolution with widescreen patches.

[SIZE=5][B]Known issues — please read[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][B]Widevine L3 only[/B] — the GT-King never had an L1 keybox (the stock firmware was L3 too). Streaming apps play in SD.
[*][B]Netflix[/B]: the Android TV app refuses uncertified devices (error -13); the Netflix phone app installs and runs.
[*][B]Chromecast built-in[/B] (receiver) cannot register — it needs a per-device certificate that only certified boxes have. Casting from apps on the box works; AirPlay/Cast receiver apps (e.g. AirScreen) work.
[*][B]Standby[/B] keeps the SoC awake (screen and HDMI off, instant wake); deep sleep is not used.
[*][B]Ethernet untested[/B] — the test unit's PHY is dead in hardware. Reports welcome.
[*]ADB over USB does not work (use ADB over network).
[*]Not certified by Google: register the device at google.com/android/uncertified if the Play Store complains.
[*][B]64-bit build[/B]: once on the test unit the first boot after flashing came up without the chain-load hook (the box switched itself off after ~12 s). The fix is one command over the internal UART header (see INSTALL.md). Please report if it happens to you.
[*][B]64-bit build[/B]: userdebug build with test-keys; the device model shows lineage_odroidn2.
[*]PAL games (50 fps) on a 60 Hz-only TV judder slightly — use a game's 60 Hz mode where available.
[/LIST]

